Third-party components
----------------------
KitePlayer decodes media through KiteFFmpeg (https://github.com/yuroyami/KiteFFmpeg),
a separate Apache-2.0 project, consumed as a dependency and not vendored into
this repository. KiteFFmpeg in turn links against FFmpeg's libav* libraries
(https://ffmpeg.org), licensed under the LGPL-2.1-or-later (or the GPL when
built with --enable-gpl). FFmpeg is NOT covered by this NOTICE or by
KitePlayer's Apache-2.0 license, and neither is KiteFFmpeg's own NOTICE.

The published KiteFFmpeg artifacts that KitePlayer depends on embed exactly
two third-party components, statically linked into the native libraries:
FFmpeg (LGPL-2.1-or-later, https://ffmpeg.org) and dav1d (BSD-2-Clause,
https://code.videolan.org/videolan/dav1d), its software AV1 decoder. KiteFFmpeg
publishes no GPL flavour, and nothing else is linked. KiteFFmpeg's NOTICE lists
them with their versions and the FFmpeg source offer. The per-component
licenses, and the distribution obligations they carry, are documented at:
https://yuroyami.github.io/KiteFFmpeg/licensing/
(source: docs/licensing.md in the KiteFFmpeg repository).

kiteplayer-libass links, statically, into its published artifacts:
libass 0.17.4 (ISC, https://github.com/libass/libass), HarfBuzz 14.2.1 (MIT,
https://github.com/harfbuzz/harfbuzz), FreeType 2.14.3 (FreeType License,
https://freetype.org) and FriBidi 1.0.16 (LGPL-2.1-or-later,
https://github.com/fribidi/fribidi). Their license texts ship inside the JVM
and Android artifacts under META-INF/licenses/kiteplayer-libass/, and inside
the web zip under licenses/. The native klibs have no place for them, so for
those the texts are in this repository, under
kiteplayer-libass/src/jvmMain/resources/META-INF/licenses/kiteplayer-libass/.
FriBidi's LGPL terms apply to those artifacts; its source is the tagged
upstream release, and the archives are built by KiteFFmpeg's
buildAssChainFor* tasks and published as release assets of that repository.

The Windows adapter in the kiteplayer-libass JVM artifact also links GNU
libiconv 1.16 (LGPL-2.0-or-later, https://www.gnu.org/software/libiconv/)
statically. The Kotlin/Native MinGW toolchain supplies it, and a Windows
executable that Kotlin/Native links against kiteplayer-libass links it the
same way. Its license text ships beside the others in the JVM artifact. Its
LGPL terms apply as FriBidi's do; its source is GNU libiconv 1.16 as MSYS2
packages it, which is where that toolchain comes from.

kiteplayer-subtitles carries decoding tables for Shift_JIS, EUC-JP, GBK, Big5
and EUC-KR. scripts/generate-east-asian-tables.py generates them from the index
files of the WHATWG Encoding Standard (https://encoding.spec.whatwg.org/).
Copyright (c) WHATWG (Apple, Google, Mozilla, Microsoft), under the BSD 3-Clause
License. The license text heads the generated file, EastAsianTables.kt, and
ships inside the JVM and Android artifacts under
META-INF/licenses/kiteplayer-subtitles/.

Visualiser drawings
-------------------
kiteplayer-audioviz ports ten browser visualisers, and it carries shader and
colour code from other open source projects. The class documentation of each
drawing credits its original and lists every way it differs. The license
texts ship inside the JVM and Android artifacts under
META-INF/licenses/kiteplayer-audioviz/. The native klibs have no place for
them, so for those the texts are in this repository, under
kiteplayer-audioviz/src/jvmMain/resources/META-INF/licenses/kiteplayer-audioviz/.

- Silk ports codevember, Copyright (c) 2015 Matt DesLauriers, MIT. It also
  carries Ashima simplex noise, Copyright (C) 2011 by Ashima Arts, MIT;
  gl-vignette-background, Copyright (c) 2014 Matt DesLauriers, MIT; and
  glsl-hsl2rgb, Copyright (c) 2015 Jam3, MIT.
- Lines ports Lines by Silvio Paganini (FLUUUID), ISC, as its package.json
  declares. The original has no license file and no copyright line.
- Honeycomb ports soundcloud-visualizer, Copyright (c) 2013 Michael Bromley,
  MIT.
- Fracture, Threads and Iris port Vissonance, Copyright (c) 2020 Tariq
  Soliman, MIT.
- Fireworks ports fireworks-webgl, Copyright (c) 2021 Ondřej Žára, MIT. It
  also re-implements functions of gl-matrix 2.2.1, Copyright (c) 2013 Brandon
  Jones, Colin MacKenzie IV, under the zlib license.
- Wavy Spiral ports Audible Visuals by Sonia Boller, Apache License 2.0. The
  original's license file leaves the copyright line unfilled.
- Muser ports Muser by Jon Shamir, MIT. Its license file reads "Copyright (c)
  2017 Matt DesLauriers". It also carries functions of chroma.js 2.1.0,
  Copyright (c) 2011-2019, Gregor Aisch, BSD 3-Clause, and the line width of
  three.meshline, Copyright (c) 2016 Jaume Sanchez, MIT.
- Musical Spectrum re-implements ShowCQTBar by Muhammad Faiz from its
  formulas. No code of it is copied, and its page has no license.

Sample media
------------
The five songs in kiteplayer-sample-shared/media are by Skullbeatz, from the
Newgrounds Audio Portal, and are licensed under Creative Commons
Attribution-ShareAlike 3.0 (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/):
bad-cat.mp3, blue.mp3, helios.mp3, hurry-up-2021.mp3 and
what-a-horrible-night.mp3. kiteplayer-sample-shared/media/README.md gives the
title and page of each one. They are not covered by KitePlayer's Apache-2.0
license. Only the sample apps use them, and no published artifact contains
them.
